The buggy method is:

com.google.javascript.rhino.jstype.RecordType.getLeastSupertype(com.google.javascript.rhino.jstype.JSType)

Diagnosis:
- The failing assertions all involve `recordType.getLeastSupertype(otherRecordType)`.
- Expected results are unions of the two record types, e.g. `({a: number, b: string}|{b: string, c: string, e: number})`.
- Actual results are either:
  - the shared-property record, e.g. `{b: string}`, or
  - `Object` when there are no shared compatible properties.

Static code confirms why:

```java
@Override
public JSType getLeastSupertype(JSType that) {
  if (!that.isRecordType()) {
    return super.getLeastSupertype(that);
  }
  RecordTypeBuilder builder = new RecordTypeBuilder(registry);
  for (String property : properties.keySet()) {
    if (that.toMaybeRecordType().hasProperty(property) &&
        that.toMaybeRecordType().getPropertyType(property).isEquivalentTo(
            getPropertyType(property))) {
      builder.addProperty(property, getPropertyType(property),
          getPropertyNode(property));
    }
  }
  return builder.build();
}
```

This method computes only the common compatible properties between the two record types. That is intersection-like behavior, not least-supertype behavior for these tests.

The tests show:
- overlapping records => actual common part `{b: ...}`
- disjoint records => actual empty record / `Object`

So the implementation is effectively returning a greatest common structural supertype, while the expected semantics for `getLeastSupertype` here are to return the union type of the two records when neither subsumes the other.

The dynamic verification was inconclusive due to limited runtime coverage, but the static evidence is strong and directly matches all observed failures.

